 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "THE DEVIL'S STAIRCASE. At about 550 metres the highest point on the West Highland Way, a zigzag up out of Kinlochleven towards Glencoe. The name is said to come from the soldiers who built the military road over it in the 1750s. They had a view of the Devil's own handiwork and no pension for it.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_staircase",
   "title": "The Devil's Staircase"
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 42,
   "text": "KINLOCHLEVEN. The village grew up round an aluminium smelter that opened in 1909 and ran on hydro-electric power from the Blackwater Reservoir above it. The smelter closed in the 1990s. The pipes still come down the hill, and the old works now hold an indoor ice-climbing centre.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_kinlochleven",
   "title": "Kinlochleven"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "THE MAMORES. A long ridge of high hills to the south, a dozen Munros one after another. Hillwalkers do the whole ridge in a day and then tell everyone about it in the pub for a year."
  }
 ],
